## std::future — Future / Poll / Context / Waker surface.
##
## Sub-phase 1 of the async round (docs/specs/stdlib/async.spec.md):
## pure stdlib surface, no executor, no `async def` lowering. The
## types declared here let user code write `include Future` /
## `type Output = T` / `def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[T]`
## and have it type-check. Running anything that calls into the
## Waker / Context C runtime panics with "executor not yet
## implemented" — sub-phase 3 fills in the real implementations.
##
## Why a separate package: the Future mixin signature references
## Poll AND Context, and Poll is generic over the future's Output.
## Co-locating the three keeps the cross-references inside one
## bootstrap-loaded file and avoids ordering surprises between
## `core.rvn` (Future mixin's old home) and the executor runtime.

## Poll[T] — the result of polling a Future. Tag layout is fixed:
##   Ready = 0, Pending = 1
## Pinned by `poll_tag_layout_stability` in
## `compiler/riven_core/tests/async_surface.rs`. DO NOT reorder;
## append new variants at the END only (no current need, kept here
## for parity with the IoError contract).
enum Poll[T]
  Ready(T)
  Pending
end

## Future — the mixin every async type implements. Sub-phase 1
## defines the surface only; sub-phase 2 lowers `async def` / `.await`
## to state machines that satisfy this shape. Implementors:
##
##   class CountdownFuture
##     remaining: Int
##     include Future
##     type Output = Int
##
##     def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Int]
##       if self.remaining == 0
##         Poll.Ready(0)
##       else
##         self.remaining = self.remaining - 1
##         Poll.Pending
##       end
##     end
##   end
mixin Future
  type Output
  def var poll(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Self.Output]
end

## Context — the executor-supplied polling context. Holds an opaque
## pointer the executor maintains; user code only ever calls
## `cx.waker` to obtain the wake handle.
##
## Sub-phase 3 of the async round (executor.spec.md B4) wired
## `cx.waker` to return a real singleton no-op Waker (no longer
## panics). The mechanical "wake" channel is inert until sub-phase
## 4 introduces genuine park points (async I/O on epoll/kqueue).
##
## Context.test_dummy: a hand-rolled no-op context used by the
## async-lowering pin tests (async_lowering.spec.md B6 — Milestone
## 2A). The dummy and the executor-made context are wire-compatible
## — both carry the singleton no-op waker, so `cx.waker()` works on
## either kind.
##
## Context.executor: factory used by the compiler intrinsic
## `block_on` rewriter (executor.spec.md B1, B4). Returns a heap-
## allocated context whose waker is the same singleton no-op as
## test_dummy. v1 sub-phase 3 has no per-task wake state, so a
## single shared instance per call is fine.
## Context — the executor-supplied polling context. Sub-phase 4A
## (docs/specs/stdlib/async_io.spec.md B3) wires the per-thread
## reactor through `Context.executor` (which allocates and installs
## the reactor on the current thread) and `Context.drop` (which
## tears it down at Context scope exit). The reactor itself is
## thread-local in the C runtime — see
## library/std/future/runtime/reactor.c — so the AST-level block_on
## rewriter's existing `Thread.yield_now` call in the Pending arm
## now drives the reactor's wait point without any compiler change.
class Context
  lib "runtime/executor.c"
    def waker as "riven_context_waker"(self) -> &Waker
    def self.test_dummy as "riven_context_test_dummy"() -> Context
    def self.executor as "riven_executor_make_context"() -> Context
    def drop as "riven_executor_context_drop"(self) -> ()
  end
end

## Waker — handle a Future stashes to notify the executor when it
## becomes pollable again. Two methods: `wake` (consumes the waker)
## and `wake_by_ref` (borrowed).
##
## Sub-phase 3 of the async round (executor.spec.md B3): both
## methods are no-ops. The poll loop in block_on spins
## continuously (with Thread.yield_now between iterations), so any
## "wake" is implicit. Sub-phase 4 wires real signaling when async
## I/O introduces genuine blocking park points.
class Waker
  lib "runtime/executor.c"
    def wake as "riven_waker_wake"(self) -> ()
    def wake_by_ref as "riven_waker_wake_by_ref"(self) -> ()
  end
end
